(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-11-01"] = {"2.1":{"s":"Physics uses two kinds of quantities. A scalar has only magnitude, such as mass, time or temperature. A vector has both magnitude and direction, such as displacement, velocity, force and acceleration. This chapter explains how to add, subtract and multiply vectors, how to break one into components, and ends with a short introduction to calculus, which is needed in later chapters."},"2.2":{"s":"Vectors are written with an arrow, and drawn as arrows whose length shows the magnitude. Some types: a zero vector has zero magnitude; the negative of a vector has the same size and opposite direction; equal vectors have the same magnitude and direction; a position vector gives the position of a point from the origin. A unit vector has magnitude one, and i, j and k are the unit vectors along x, y and z.","f":[{"x":"u = M/|M|","n":"Unit vector along a vector M: the vector divided by its magnitude.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.2, p.17"}],"w":["A unit vector has no unit: it only gives direction."]},"2.3":{"s":"Vectors are added by the triangle law (place the tail of the second at the head of the first) or by the parallelogram law (draw both from the same point and take the diagonal). Vector addition is commutative and associative. Subtracting a vector means adding its negative. Multiplying a vector by a scalar changes its magnitude (and reverses its direction if the scalar is negative). The size of a sum depends on the angle between the vectors.","f":[{"x":"R = sqrt(P^2 + Q^2 + 2*P*Q*cos(theta))","n":"Magnitude of the resultant of two vectors P and Q with angle theta between them (tails together).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.3, p.20"},{"x":"tan(alpha) = Q*sin(theta)/(P + Q*cos(theta))","n":"Direction of the resultant: alpha = angle it makes with P.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.3, p.20"}],"w":["The magnitude of a sum is not the sum of magnitudes, unless the vectors point the same way.","For θ = 0° the resultant is P + Q; for θ = 180° it is |P − Q|; for θ = 90° it is √(P² + Q²)."]},"2.4":{"s":"A vector can be split into components along chosen directions, usually along perpendicular axes x and y. This is resolution of a vector. For a vector of magnitude R at an angle θ to the x axis, the x component is R cos θ and the y component is R sin θ. Adding or subtracting vectors becomes easy: add the x components together and the y components together. Then rebuild the magnitude and direction.","f":[{"x":"R_x = R*cos(theta), R_y = R*sin(theta)","n":"Rectangular components of a vector R at angle theta to the x axis.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.4, p.21"},{"x":"R = sqrt(R_x^2 + R_y^2)","n":"Magnitude from the components.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.4, p.21"},{"x":"tan(theta) = R_y/R_x","n":"Direction from the components.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.4, p.21"}],"w":["θ is measured from the axis you choose: if you measure from the y axis, sine and cosine swap places."]},"2.5":{"s":"There are two ways to multiply two vectors. The scalar (dot) product gives a number: P·Q = PQ cos θ. It is zero for perpendicular vectors, and in components it is PxQx + PyQy + PzQz. The vector (cross) product gives a vector perpendicular to both, with magnitude PQ sin θ and direction from the right hand rule. It is not commutative: P×Q = −(Q×P). Its size is the area of the parallelogram on the two vectors.","f":[{"x":"P.Q = P*Q*cos(theta) = P_x*Q_x + P_y*Q_y + P_z*Q_z","n":"Scalar (dot) product; zero when the vectors are perpendicular, and P.P = P^2.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.5, p.23"},{"x":"|P x Q| = P*Q*sin(theta)","n":"Magnitude of the vector (cross) product; zero for parallel vectors.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.5, p.24"},{"x":"F = q*(v x B)","n":"Force on a charge q moving with velocity v in a magnetic field B: an example of a cross product.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.5, p.24"},{"x":"Area of parallelogram = |P x Q|","n":"Magnitude of the cross product = area of the parallelogram with sides P and Q.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.5, p.25"},{"x":"L = r x p","n":"Angular momentum: cross product of the position vector r and the linear momentum p.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.5, p.25"}],"w":["i × j = k, j × k = i, k × i = j; reverse the order and the sign changes.","Dot product of perpendicular vectors is 0; cross product of parallel vectors is 0."]},"2.6":{"s":"Calculus studies how quantities change. Differential calculus finds the rate of change of one quantity with respect to another, as the slope of the tangent to a graph; this is the derivative. Integral calculus does the reverse and finds areas. A few rules help: the derivative of a sum is the sum of the derivatives, plus the product rule, the quotient rule and the chain rule for a function of a function.","f":[{"x":"dy/dx = lim (delta x -> 0) of delta y/delta x","n":"The derivative: the limit of the ratio of the change in y to the change in x; the slope of the tangent.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.6, p.26"},{"x":"d(f_1*f_2)/dx = f_1*d(f_2)/dx + f_2*d(f_1)/dx","n":"Product rule.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.6, p.26"},{"x":"d/dx f(g(x)) = f'(g(x))*g'(x)","n":"Chain rule for a function of a function.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 2.6, p.26"}],"w":["Do not forget the inner derivative in the chain rule."]}};
